I'm inclined to leave Rodney, Moloch, and the Endgame as they are.

General game idea: alignment should have a max value, possibly the same as the
minimum value for being piously aligned.  So instead of ending up massively
piously aligned so that breaking alignment doesn't matter even for a knight,
you'll have to pay attention and be careful.  A reward of being crowned would
be to boost the max by 5 or 10 points, giving you more breathing space.

Drawback of being crowned is that when you emerge with the Amulet you get met
by a crowned NPC with magic prot, decent resistances, and the appropriate
crowned weapon (which would no longer be wishable.)  If you're the Hand, you
meet the Envoy of Balance with Vorpal Blade.  If you're the Envoy, you meet
the Glory of Arioch with Stormbringer.  If you're the Glory you meet the Hand
with Excalibur.  Yes, this is targetting the weaknesses...  if Excalibur or
Stormbringer weren't generated then maybe 50% chance of who meets you to claim
the Amulet for their god.

Maybe decrease the prayer timeout increase so being crowned sucks less.

Vlad's already been toughened, but I was inspired by the original and thought
he should have 3 succubie in his tower, whom you can meet all at once.  Help
male characters off with that armor...  for female chars, Vlad himself has an
incubus attack, except the consequence is always losing a level.  And Vlad
summons fog vortices.

Another idea: Arawn Death-Lord, an ungenocidable arch-lich with a bunch of
zombie, maybe an artifact which makes pet zombies (a la the Black Cauldron) at
cost to alignment, and the Huntsmen, just a band of ranger-like characters.
Who gain level every time one of them is killed.

In general, my feeling for spicing up Gehennom would be to tilt more to open
levels, with mazes for the Wizard's Towers levels.  But it might be felt this
would be too like the elemental planes.  Different mix of monsters though
(demons and devils and ghosts and undead) and perhaps more like the Gnomish
Mines in layout.

